{
  "verdict": "revise",
  "scores": {
    "novelty": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Ordinal generational depth scoring is genuinely novel — no competing work found across extensive search. However, arXiv:2502.15654 (Drayson et al., Feb 2025) directly addresses collapse prevention via binary DeBERTa-v3 detection + importance resampling of human content, and is not cited; the differentiation (binary vs. ordinal depth, resampling vs. tiered filtering) is clear but must be made explicit."
    },
    "feasibility": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "Gen1 vs Gen2 discrimination from the same 0.6B model remains the central hard risk — the Wall of Separation finding (arXiv:2605.01647) makes within-model generational discrimination harder than cross-model AI-AI divergence, and the embedding-drift mitigation is hypothesized but unvalidated; the dual null hypothesis de-risks publication but not technical success, and the 183/200 H100h compute margin in auto mode is dangerously thin if any training run requires restart."
    },
    "clarity": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Exceptionally well-documented with explicit day-by-day critical path, preregistered dual null hypothesis covering all four logical outcomes, and explicit differentiation from ForTIFAI, TrainProVe, and hierarchical contamination detection; the missing arXiv:2502.15654 citation is the only notable gap in completeness."
    },
    "impact": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Near-perfect topical alignment with EMNLP 2026 special theme; all four outcome combinations produce interpretable governance findings; the GenDepth-Bench contribution is concrete; depth-stratified filtering addresses a genuine practical governance question that binary detection (2502.15654) leaves unanswered."
    }
  },
  "issues": [
    "MISSING CITATION — arXiv:2502.15654 'Machine-generated text detection prevents language model collapse' (Drayson, Yilmaz, Lampos; UCL, Feb 2025) is the most directly adjacent prior work: it trains a binary DeBERTa-v3 classifier to distinguish human vs AI text, applies importance resampling to up-weight human content during recursive training, and demonstrates collapse prevention at GPT-2/SmolLM2 scale. This paper is not cited anywhere in the proposal. A reviewer will immediately flag this omission since it directly addresses the same problem (AI text detection to prevent collapse). The differentiation is clear — 2502.15654 uses binary detection and does not distinguish generational depth; the proposed method extends this to ordinal depth scoring enabling tiered filtering — but it must be cited and explicitly differentiated.",
    "COMPUTE MARGIN TOO THIN — Auto research at ~183 H100h equivalent leaves only 17h of buffer against the 200h hard constraint. The contingency trigger at 180h H100-equivalent is well-specified (drop Llama-3.2-1B recursive corpus to zero-shot only), but this saves 20-30h H100-equivalent — meaning the contingency must activate before the budget is exceeded by more than ~3h. Any single failed training run or unexpected GPU contention during the sequential corpus construction bottleneck (Days 2-4) could push total usage past 200h. The proposal should either (a) declare the Llama-3.2-1B recursive corpus as optional from the start with the contingency trigger moved earlier (e.g., at 160h), or (b) estimate auto research at a slightly lower total."
  ],
  "feedback": "Both Round 3 issues are properly resolved — the secondary null hypothesis (AUC ≥ 0.65 but collapse severity parity) is now explicitly preregistered with a paired t-test criterion and governance interpretation, and the n-gram-to-transformer modeling gap is acknowledged with the NeurIPS 2024 grounding. Two targeted fixes will bring this to pass: (1) Add arXiv:2502.15654 to the landscape and key_references sections. The differentiation sentence writes itself: 'The closest upstream related work (arXiv:2502.15654, Feb 2025) shows that binary AI-text detection via DeBERTa-v3 with importance resampling prevents collapse at GPT-2/SmolLM2 scale, but treats all AI-generated text equivalently regardless of generational depth — leaving open whether depth stratification enables more efficient decontamination per filtering unit, which is the specific question we answer.' (2) Move the Llama-3.2-1B recursive corpus to explicitly optional from the start in auto_research, and set the contingency activation threshold at 160h H100-equivalent rather than 180h, creating a more credible 40h safety margin. These two changes require no new experiments.",
  "reason": "All Round 3 issues addressed, core novelty verified, but missing arXiv:2502.15654 (the closest prior work on binary detection for collapse prevention) creates a related-work gap that reviewers will flag, and the compute buffer is dangerously thin at 17h H100-equivalent."
}
